Hitchhiking is legal in Northern Ireland and no law forbids asking for a lift; only the motorways are closed to you, under regulations of their own rather than the ones covering Great Britain. [https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisr/2008/135/made The Motorways Traffic Regulations (Northern Ireland) 2008] treat pedestrians as excluded traffic and regulation 13(1)(b) allows them on a motorway only where an accident, an emergency or a vehicle at rest makes it necessary — so the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads of the M1, M2 and the rest are out. Drivers cannot legally stop for you there either, the same regulations restricting stopping to emergencies and the places provided. Northern Ireland has a short motorway network and a long A-road one, and on the A-roads hitchhiking is unregulated. Stand off the carriageway, on the verge, where a driver can see you and pull in safely.
